# TODO — Next LSA Architecture: Capability-Driven Per-Module Compatibility

## What I'm Working On Next

The next major LSA refactor is changing compatibility detection from:

```text
"What Android version / phone is this?"
```

to:

```text
"What can this device actually do?"
```

LSA currently has several working compatibility systems, but many were developed individually as the project expanded.

The next step is to formalize them into a common architecture where **each major subsystem independently probes the device, chooses the best implementation available, and reports exactly what it selected.**

This will become the foundation for making LSA portable across devices I have never tested before.

---

# The Main Idea

LSA will no longer classify an entire phone as something like:

```text
Tier A phone
Tier B phone
Tier C phone
```

Instead, **every module gets its own independent compatibility tier.**

A device might look like:

```text
Core chroot         TIER A / AVAILABLE
Device namespace    TIER B / AVAILABLE
Storage             TIER A / AVAILABLE
System D-Bus        TIER A / AVAILABLE
Display             TIER C / AVAILABLE
GPU                 TIER C / AVAILABLE
Audio               TIER A / AVAILABLE
Camera              TIER C / AVAILABLE
Sensors             TIER D / AVAILABLE
```

Those letters are local to each subsystem.

For example:

```text
Audio Tier A
    AAudio / HALAudioS

Sensors Tier A
    native usable Linux IIO

GPU Tier A
    directly usable native GPU interface
```

These technologies have nothing to do with each other.

`Tier A` simply means:

> The best implementation LSA currently knows how to use for that specific subsystem.

---

# Why I'm Doing This

Android devices are inconsistent.

Two phones running the same Android version may have very different:

* kernels
* GPU drivers
* device nodes
* FUSE behavior
* sensor implementations
* audio capabilities
* camera interfaces
* SELinux rules
* vendor modifications

Because of that, this:

```text
Android 13
    ↓
use backend X
```

is not reliable enough.

Neither is:

```text
Blackview N6000
    ↓
use backend Y
```

The new architecture will instead do:

```text
probe capability
      ↓
actually test it
      ↓
AVAILABLE / UNSUPPORTED / ERROR
      ↓
use it or try the next backend
```

Android version, API level, kernel version, manufacturer, architecture, model, and SELinux mode will still be recorded.

They just will not be the primary source of truth.

---

# Standard Tier Result

Every backend probe should return one of three primary states:

## AVAILABLE

The backend was actually tested and works.

```text
FUSE backend

/dev/fuse opened
temporary mount succeeded
required options succeeded
cleanup succeeded

AVAILABLE
```

---

## UNSUPPORTED

The required capability genuinely does not exist.

```text
CUSE backend

/dev/cuse absent
kernel CUSE support unavailable

UNSUPPORTED
```

This is normal.

It does not mean the phone is broken.

---

## ERROR

The capability appears to exist, but something unexpected prevented it from working.

```text
Software IIO backend

IIO support detected
configfs detected
temporary device creation attempted

EINVAL

ERROR
```

Errors will remain visible for debugging, but they should normally **not stop lower tiers from being attempted.**

Example:

```text
Sensors

Tier A   UNSUPPORTED
Tier B   ERROR
Tier C   UNSUPPORTED
Tier D   AVAILABLE

Selected:
Tier D
```

---

# Capability Graph

Another important change is that modules will stop depending directly on each other's tier letters.

Bad:

```text
Sensors Tier D requires DeviceNamespace Tier B
```

The sensor implementation should not care how DeviceNamespace created a private `/dev`.

Instead:

```text
Sensors Tier D requires:

capability.sensorbus
capability.fuse.mount
capability.device.private
capability.unix_socket
```

DeviceNamespace might provide:

```text
capability.device.private
```

through Tier A on one phone and Tier B on another.

Sensors only cares that the required capability exists.

This turns LSA into a **capability graph instead of a hardcoded implementation graph.**

---

# Planned Module Layout

The eventual structure will look roughly like:

```text
LSA/
│
├── Platform/
│   ├── probes
│   ├── capability database
│   ├── selectors
│   └── platform report
│
├── Audio/
│   ├── selector
│   └── tiers/
│
├── GPU/
│   ├── selector
│   └── tiers/
│
├── Camera/
│   └── tiers/
│
├── Sensors/
│   └── tiers/
│
├── DeviceNamespace/
│   └── tiers/
│
├── Storage/
│   └── tiers/
│
├── Display/
│   └── tiers/
│
└── DBus/
    └── tiers/
```

Every module should conceptually support:

```text
probe
start
stop
status
provides
requires
```

Not every module needs separate scripts for all of these immediately.

This is the interface LSA will gradually standardize around.

---

# First Module: Sensors

Sensors will be the first subsystem converted to the new architecture.

Why sensors?

Because the current sensor stack already demonstrates exactly why capability-based fallback is useful.

The planned ladder is approximately:

```text
SENSORS

Tier A
    native usable Linux IIO

Tier B
    kernel-assisted/software IIO
    HALSensorBus → kernel IIO devices

Tier C
    CUSE-backed IIO compatibility

Tier D
    FUSE-backed synthetic IIO
    private /dev
    userspace brokers
    event ioctl compatibility

Tier E
    high-level Linux sensor compatibility
    D-Bus / SensorProxy

Tier F
    LSA-native sensor API
```

The current known-good N6000 implementation is:

```text
Tier A   UNSUPPORTED
Tier B   UNSUPPORTED
Tier C   UNSUPPORTED
Tier D   AVAILABLE
Tier F   AVAILABLE

Selected:
Tier D
```

The existing Tier-D implementation is already working and **will not be rewritten during the initial selector work.**

The order is:

```text
build selector
      ↓
prove selector
      ↓
wrap existing Tier D
      ↓
verify behavior remains identical
```

Known-good functionality stays known-good.

---

# Device Namespace

The private `/dev` work will become its own module shortly after sensors.

Possible ladder:

```text
DEVICE NAMESPACE

Tier A
    native safe Linux device namespace

Tier B
    private tmpfs /dev
    Android devices under /dev/.host
    selected compatibility links/devices exposed to Linux

Tier C
    selective device recreation/proxying

Tier D
    userspace device proxies

UNSUPPORTED
```

The current N6000 private `/dev` implementation is already proven and will likely become the current Tier B backend.

---

# Audio

Audio gets its own independent ladder.

Current direction:

```text
AUDIO

Tier A
    current AAudio / HALAudioS implementation

Tier C
    SoX compatibility fallback

additional tiers
    added only when real implementations exist
```

I am deliberately **not filling every letter just because A–Z exists.**

Unused tiers are fine.

A backend gets a tier when there is actually something worth putting there.

---

# GPU

The GPU module will eventually choose between things such as:

```text
GPU

Tier A
    directly usable native Linux GPU path

Tier B
    high-quality Android GPU adaptation

Tier C
    GPUBridge / VirGL / virpipe

Tier D
    software Mesa / llvmpipe

Tier E
    remote/framebuffer rendering

Tier F
    no acceleration
```

The exact tier definitions will be finalized around real implementations and real hardware probes.

---

# Display

Likely direction:

```text
DISPLAY

Tier A
    native DRM/KMS

Tier B
    native or bridged Wayland

Tier C
    Termux:X11

Tier D
    VNC / RFB

Tier E
    generic framebuffer transport
```

Again, this is a direction, not a promise that every tier will exist.

---

# Camera

Likely direction:

```text
CAMERA

Tier A
    usable native V4L2

Tier B
    Android camera → V4L2-style bridge

Tier C
    HALAgent camera/frame bridge

Tier D
    GStreamer / PipeWire compatibility

Tier E
    LSA-native camera API
```

---

# FUSE

FUSE also needs real capability detection.

Checking:

```text
/dev/fuse exists
```

is not enough.

LSA needs to know what that FUSE implementation can actually do.

Possible capabilities include:

```text
capability.fuse.mount
capability.fuse.direct_io
capability.fuse.sysfs_overlay
```

A probe should therefore test the actual operation LSA needs.

Example:

```text
open /dev/fuse
      ↓
create temporary filesystem
      ↓
mount it
      ↓
test required behavior
      ↓
unmount
      ↓
clean everything up
```

Only then is the capability marked:

```text
AVAILABLE
```

---

# Platform Report

A major visible result of this refactor will be a centralized:

```text
LSA Platform Report
```

Example:

```text
LSA Platform Report
────────────────────────────────────────

Android             13 / API 33
Architecture        arm64
Kernel              5.10.x
SELinux             permissive

Core chroot         TIER A / AVAILABLE
Device namespace    TIER B / AVAILABLE
Storage             TIER A / AVAILABLE
System D-Bus        TIER A / AVAILABLE
Display             TIER C / AVAILABLE
GPU                 TIER C / AVAILABLE
Audio               TIER A / AVAILABLE
Camera              TIER C / AVAILABLE
Sensors             TIER D / AVAILABLE

Overall support      FULL
```

The Android information is descriptive.

The module results are authoritative.

---

# Verbose Platform Report

There will also be a diagnostic mode.

Example:

```text
Sensors
────────────────────────────────────────

Tier A
    UNSUPPORTED

    native IIO devices: 2
    suitable sensor mappings: 0/20

Tier B
    UNSUPPORTED

    CONFIG_IIO: yes
    configfs: yes
    usable software IIO devices: no

Tier C
    UNSUPPORTED

    CUSE: unavailable

Tier D
    AVAILABLE

    HALSensorBus: yes
    FUSE mount: yes
    private /dev: yes
    synthetic mappings: 20/20
    event shim: yes

Selected:
    Tier D / fuse-iio
```

Unexpected failures should show useful diagnostics:

```text
Tier B
    ERROR

    probe:
        temporary IIO device creation

    errno:
        EINVAL
```

---

# Runtime State

LSA will record its selections instead of trying to rediscover everything whenever another module asks.

Planned layout:

```text
/run/lsa/
│
├── platform.json
├── capabilities.json
│
└── modules/
    ├── audio.json
    ├── gpu.json
    ├── camera.json
    ├── sensors.json
    ├── storage.json
    └── device-namespace.json
```

Example:

```json
{
  "module": "sensors",
  "status": "AVAILABLE",
  "selected_tier": "D",
  "backend": "fuse-iio",
  "provides": [
    "linux.iio.sysfs",
    "linux.iio.chardev",
    "linux.iio.events"
  ]
}
```

Other modules can then consume the capabilities rather than inspecting implementation internals.

---

# Startup Direction

Eventually LSA startup should roughly become:

```text
collect platform facts
        ↓
probe primitive capabilities
        ↓
build capability graph
        ↓
probe module tiers
        ↓
select best AVAILABLE backend
        ↓
resolve dependencies
        ↓
start selected implementations
        ↓
verify runtime health
        ↓
write runtime state
        ↓
generate Platform Report
```

Teardown becomes:

```text
read recorded runtime state
        ↓
determine active implementations
        ↓
stop modules in reverse dependency order
        ↓
remove mounts/sockets/runtime state
        ↓
clean shutdown
```

LSA should not blindly probe the machine again during shutdown when it already knows what it started.

---

# Development Order

## Phase 1 — Capability Framework

Build the common foundation:

* capability result format
* module result format
* `AVAILABLE / UNSUPPORTED / ERROR`
* tier selector
* dependency system
* `provides` / `requires`
* runtime state directory
* Platform Report generator

Existing implementations stay where they are during this phase.

---

## Phase 2 — Sensors

Sensors become the first complete tiered subsystem.

Build probes for the planned sensor backends.

Current phone must still resolve to:

```text
Sensors
Tier D / AVAILABLE
```

and launch the existing known-good implementation.

---

## Phase 3 — Wrap Existing Tier-D Sensors

Move or wrap the current:

* LSAIIORegistry
* LSAIIOProxy
* StreamBroker
* StateBroker
* EventBroker
* event ioctl shim
* sensor runtime controller

behind:

```text
Sensors/tiers/D/
```

No functional rewrite.

Behavior should remain identical.

---

## Phase 4 — Device Namespace

Convert the current private `/dev` implementation into the same modular system.

Preserve the current working behavior.

---

## Phase 5 — Audio

Formalize the current audio implementations and their probes.

Start with real backends that already exist.

Do not invent tiers just to populate the alphabet.

---

## Phase 6 — GPU

Formalize:

* native GPU possibilities
* GPUBridge
* VirGL / virpipe
* software rendering
* other fallback paths

as independent implementations.

---

## Phase 7 — Camera

Turn native and bridged camera paths into independent backends.

---

## Phase 8 — Remaining Modules

Apply the architecture to:

* Storage
* Display
* System D-Bus
* FUSE
* networking-related compatibility
* other hardware/service modules

---

## Phase 9 — Remove Old Version Gating

Audit uses of:

```text
getprop
Android API level
Android release
manufacturer
device model
kernel version
```

Replace implementation decisions with real probes wherever possible.

Keep platform facts where they are genuinely required or useful for diagnostics.

---

## Phase 10 — Overall Support Status

Human-friendly statuses may remain:

```text
FULL
PARTIAL
MINIMAL
UNSUPPORTED
```

but they will be **derived from module results**.

Example:

```text
FULL
    required core modules available

PARTIAL
    core available
    optional hardware compatibility degraded

MINIMAL
    basic Linux environment works
    major integrations unavailable

UNSUPPORTED
    required core capabilities unavailable
```

The module report remains the source of truth.

---

## Phase 11 — Unknown Device Testing

The important test is not whether LSA works on another phone already listed in the code.

The important test is whether LSA can encounter hardware it has never seen before and correctly figure it out.

Testing will include:

* the current N6000
* clean Debian rootfs
* missing optional dependencies
* deliberately disabled high tiers
* broken probe behavior
* fallback after `ERROR`
* fallback after `UNSUPPORTED`
* partially failed startup
* teardown after partial startup
* restart
* stale runtime state
* unknown manufacturers
* unknown Android releases
* different kernels

---

## Phase 12 — New Known-Good Snapshot

Once the architecture has proven itself, create another complete LSA known-good auto-deployment snapshot.

---

# Rules I Do Not Want This Refactor To Break

1. **The current known-good configuration must keep working.**

2. Working implementations should be wrapped before they are rewritten.

3. Every subsystem owns its own tier definitions.

4. Tier letters describe implementation preference, not universal technology classes.

5. Modules communicate through capabilities instead of depending on another module's tier letter.

6. Device model and Android version do not replace capability probing.

7. `UNSUPPORTED` and `ERROR` mean different things.

8. A broken high-tier backend should normally allow fallback.

9. Probes should test real behavior, not just filenames or kernel config flags.

10. Probes must clean up after themselves.

11. Runtime state must record what LSA actually selected.

12. Platform reports must describe reality instead of assumptions.

13. Empty tier letters are allowed.

14. Do not create fake compatibility paths just to make the tier list look complete.

---

# Where This Is Going

The desired experience on an unknown rooted Android device is eventually:

```text
LSA:
"I have never seen this phone before."

"Let's see what it can do."
```

Then LSA probes:

```text
kernel
mount behavior
device namespaces
FUSE
GPU
audio
camera
sensors
IIO
D-Bus
display
storage
Android services
...
```

and builds the system that best matches what is actually available.

The result might be:

```text
LSA Platform Report
────────────────────────────────────────

Device namespace    TIER B / AVAILABLE
Storage             TIER A / AVAILABLE
System D-Bus        TIER A / AVAILABLE
Display             TIER C / AVAILABLE
GPU                 TIER C / AVAILABLE
Audio               TIER A / AVAILABLE
Camera              TIER C / AVAILABLE
Sensors             TIER D / AVAILABLE

Overall support      FULL
```

The fact that the phone model, kernel build, vendor, or Android release has never been tested before should not automatically matter.

If the required primitives work, LSA should discover them.

If the preferred implementation does not work, LSA should fall back.

If something behaves incorrectly, LSA should report exactly what failed.

## End Goal

**LSA should adapt to the hardware and kernel it finds, not to a database of phones it already knows about.**
